<div class="Section1">

<h1>AIML CASESTUDY REPORT</h1>
<h2>TITLE: AGRIAGENT: A REAL-DATA DRIVEN AGRICULTURAL INTELLIGENCE AND FARMER DECISION SUPPORT SYSTEM FOR INDIA</h2>
<p class="repo-link"><b>GITHUB REPOSITORY:</b> <a href="https://github.com/SaiiieSJaswanth/AGRIAGENT" target="_blank">https://github.com/SaiiieSJaswanth/AGRIAGENT</a></p>
<hr>
<h3>1. INTRODUCTION</h3>
<p>Agriculture is the primary livelihood for more than 50 percent of India's population and contributes roughly 18 percent to India's Gross Domestic Product (GDP). The agricultural sector is predominantly composed of small and marginal farmers who cultivate less than two hectares of land. Every cropping season, smallholders make four crucial operational decisions:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>1.</b> Crop Selection: Choosing the most suitable crop for their local agro-climatic conditions.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>2.</b> Soil Fertility &amp; Nutrition: Determining chemical fertilizer requirements without degrading topsoil health.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>3.</b> Water Management: Scheduling irrigation to avoid groundwater depletion or waterlogging.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>4.</b> Welfare Assistance: Identifying eligible government subsidies to lower input costs.</p>
<p>In practice, smallholders rely heavily on informal guesswork, advice from local chemical retailers, or traditional habits. This leads to continuous monocropping, acute soil acidification from excessive urea usage, groundwater over-extraction, and missed welfare subsidies.</p>
<p>Machine Learning (ML), Artificial Intelligence (AI), and Data Science offer practical solutions to these issues. By learning patterns from historical government crop census records, 100-year rainfall normals, laboratory soil test assays, and crop water balance principles, data-driven systems provide reliable agronomic guidance.</p>
<p>This project implements AGRIAGENT, an integrated agricultural intelligence system designed for Indian agriculture. AGRIAGENT avoids synthetic data. It connects 233,516 official crop census records from the Directorate of Economics and Statistics (DESAgri), 641 district rainfall normals from the India Meteorological Department (IMD), 1,070 laboratory soil test samples from the National Soil Health Card scheme, ICAR guidelines, and verified government welfare initiatives into a practical advisory platform.</p>
<p>The complete open-source implementation, reproducible data pipelines, and machine learning models are available on GitHub:</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/SaiiieSJaswanth/AGRIAGENT" target="_blank">https://github.com/SaiiieSJaswanth/AGRIAGENT</a></p>
<hr>
<h3>2. MOTIVATION</h3>
<p>The motivation for this case study comes from the persistent economic and environmental vulnerabilities facing smallholder Indian farmers.</p>
<p>Data from NITI Aayog, ICAR, and the National Sample Survey Office (NSSO) highlight critical issues:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>1.</b> Smallholder Vulnerability: Over 85 percent of operational landholdings belong to small and marginal farmers, with an average operational holding size of 1.08 hectares.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>2.</b> Distorted Soil Nutrient Ratios: The scientifically recommended N:P:K balance for Indian soil health is 4:2:1. Subsidized urea has widened the national ratio to 6.7:2.4:1, and in intensive agricultural states like Punjab and Haryana, it exceeds 25:6:1. This chemical excess acidifies topsoil and locks up essential micronutrients.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>3.</b> Groundwater Depletion: According to the Central Ground Water Board (CGWB), over 60 percent of irrigated farming depends on groundwater. Unscheduled flood irrigation has driven water tables to critical depths in states like Karnataka, Rajasthan, Maharashtra, and Tamil Nadu.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20pt; text-indent: -20pt;"><b>4.</b> Welfare Information Gap: Although central and state governments allocate substantial funds for agricultural schemes (e.g., PM-KISAN, PMFBY, KCC, micro-irrigation), smallholders often fail to access these benefits due to fragmented information and complex eligibility criteria.</p>
<p>An AI and Data Science solution is needed because no farmer or local extension officer can manually evaluate twenty years of district census statistics, climate normals, multi-parameter soil chemistry, fertilizer stoichiometry, and welfare eligibility criteria simultaneously. AGRIAGENT automates this analysis to provide clear, actionable advisory.</p>
<hr>
